          "p": 11,
          "text": "Abstract"
        },
        {
          "p": 12,
          "text": "The regular dodecahedron is the forced carrier of the projection operator Π, established by the Fragility Topology (Theorems 1–16). The regular icosahedron is its dual action topology. The pentagonal faces of the dodecahedron are the semantic domains. This paper identifies a recursion: the pentagon assembles into the dodecahedron; the dodecahedron assembles into the 120-cell (the regular 4-polytope whose 120 cells are dodecahedra); the 120-cell admits no further regular convex extension. The recursion has depth 3 and terminates."
        },
        {
          "p": 13,
          "text": "The dual recursion (triangle, icosahedron, 600-cell) terminates at the same depth. The governance margin μ, defined as dual-separation under shared symmetry, is shown to be non-compositional over cellular decomposition: 120-cell cells have μ > 0 and the whole has μ > 0; 600-cell cells have μ = 0 and the whole has μ > 0; 24-cell cells have μ > 0 and the whole has μ = 0. The 24-cell is the unique self-dual non-simplex regular convex 4-polytope and exists only at the terminal recursive depth."
        },
        {
          "p": 14,
          "text": "The depth of the recursion (3) equals the cardinality of the TRIAD (□G, □S, □F). These derive independently: the recursion from the Coxeter classification of regular polytopes, the TRIAD from Axiom 1 (irreducibility of the three modalities of projection). The correspondence is established; a common-root theorem remains open."
        },
        {
          "p": 15,
          "text": "Keywords: projection operator, dodecahedral recursion, 120-cell, 600-cell, 24-cell, governance margin, non-compositionality, TRIAD, depth-3 termination, H₄ symmetry"
        },
        {
          "p": 16,
          "text": "0. Primitives"
        },
        {
          "p": 17,
          "text": "D0.1 (Projection Operator). Π := the projection operator. Every persistent entity E is a section of Π: Π(E) = (□G(E), □S(E), □F(E)). The TRIAD is irreducible (Axiom 1)."
        },
        {
          "p": 18,
