        },
        {
          "p": 50,
          "text": "Every regular polygon is self-dual. The dual of an n-gon is an n-gon with the same symmetry group. No polygon admits a nontrivial symmetry-invariant disjoint carrier decomposition between itself and its dual, because itself and its dual are the same object. μ = 0 universally at depth 1."
        },
        {
          "p": 51,
          "text": "3.2 Depth 2"
        },
        {
          "p": 52,
          "text": "The five Platonic solids partition into self-dual (tetrahedron) and non-self-dual pairs (cube/octahedron, dodecahedron/icosahedron). The Governance Margin Theorem (Generally 2026) proves μ(tetrahedron) = 0 and μ(dodecahedron) > 0. The dodecahedron/icosahedron duality provides the maintenance/action separation: dodecahedral faces carry maintenance (fragility topology), icosahedral vertices carry action (action topology). The tetrahedron is the depth-2 anomaly: the unique self-dual Platonic solid."
