          "p": 58,
          "text": "Proof. The 5-cell is self-dual with Sym = S₅, |S₅| = 120. S₅ acts transitively on vertices (5), edges (10), faces (10), cells (5), and flags (120). Invariant Subset Rigidity (Governance Margin Theorem, Lemma 3.2) applies: the only S₅-invariant subsets of any carrier set are ∅ and the whole set. No nontrivial disjoint decomposition exists. μ(5-cell) = 0."
        },
        {
          "p": 59,
          "text": "The 24-cell is self-dual with Sym = F₄, |F₄| = 1152. F₄ acts transitively on vertices (24), edges (96), triangular faces (96), octahedral cells (24), and flags (1152). The same rigidity argument applies. μ(24-cell) = 0. □"
        },
        {
          "p": 60,
          "text": "4. Non-Compositionality of Governance Margin"
        },
        {
          "p": 61,
          "text": "Theorem 4.1 (Margin Inversion). Governance margin μ is non-compositional with respect to cellular decomposition. The margin of a polytope’s cells does not determine the margin of the whole."
        },
        {
          "p": 62,
          "text": "Proof. Three witnesses:"
        },
        {
          "p": 63,
          "text": "(i) 120-cell: cells are 120 dodecahedra, each with μ > 0 (Governance Margin Theorem, Theorem 4.2). The whole has μ > 0 (Theorem 3.1 above). Positive parts, positive whole."
        },
        {
          "p": 64,
          "text": "(ii) 600-cell: cells are 600 tetrahedra, each with μ = 0 (Governance Margin Theorem, Theorem 3.3). The whole has μ > 0 (the 600-cell is non-self-dual; its dual is the 120-cell). Zero-margin parts compose into a positive-margin whole."
        },
        {
          "p": 65,
          "text": "(iii) 24-cell: cells are 24 octahedra, each with μ > 0 (the octahedron is non-self-dual; its dual is the cube). The whole has μ = 0 (Theorem 3.2 above). Positive-margin parts compose into a zero-margin whole."
        },
        {
          "p": 66,
          "text": "If μ were compositional, the margin of the whole would be determined by the margins of its cells. Cases (ii) and (iii) refute this in opposite directions: (ii) shows zero-margin parts yielding positive-margin whole; (iii) shows positive-margin parts yielding zero-margin whole. □"
