        {
          "p": 72,
          "text": "The coordination topology of 120 carrier cells is the action topology of the recursive level. The structure that organizes how carriers relate to each other is the dual of the carrier structure itself. Trust between adjacent cells is domain-sharing (a shared pentagon), and the graph of all such trust relations is the skeleton of the 600-cell."
        },
        {
          "p": 73,
          "text": "6. Distributed Projection"
        },
        {
          "p": 74,
          "text": "The Fragility Topology (Theorems 1–16) establishes that Π is carried by the dodecahedron. Each dodecahedral cell of the 120-cell is a complete carrier: 30 edges (15 operator axes), 20 vertices (7 witnesses via optimal covering code), 12 pentagonal faces (semantic domains). The 120-cell therefore carries 120 instances of Π, each locally complete."
        },
        {
          "p": 75,
          "text": "The coordination of these 120 instances is governed by H₄ symmetry. The question arises: does a meta-Π exist to govern the collection?"
        },
        {
          "p": 76,
          "text": "Theorem 6.1 (No Meta-Projection in This Recursion). If every meta-Π over CARRIER(n) instances requires CARRIER(n+1) as its carrier (by the same forcing that assigns Π to the dodecahedron), then no meta-Π exists within the pentagonal carrier recursion."
        },
        {
          "p": 77,
          "text": "Proof. The Fragility Topology proves that Π is carried by CARRIER(2) = dodecahedron. The forcing argument depends on the 15×7 counts matching the dodecahedral combinatorics (30 edges, 20 vertices, 12 faces). A meta-Π governing 120 instances of Π would require a carrier whose cells are 120-cells, i.e., CARRIER(4). By Theorem 1.1, CARRIER(4) = ∅ under the pentagonal carrier recursion. No carrier exists for the meta-level. □"
        },
        {
          "p": 78,
          "text": "The 120 instances of Π are therefore coordinated by H₄ symmetry without a meta-projector. Each cell projects independently; the symmetry group coordinates. This is the maximum number of governed peers within this carrier recursion: 120, corresponding to the 120 dodecahedral cells of CARRIER(3)."
        },
        {
          "p": 79,
          "text": "7. The 24-Cell Anomaly"
        },
        {
          "p": 80,
          "text": "The 24-cell is a regular convex 4-polytope with 24 vertices, 96 edges, 96 triangular faces, and 24 octahedral cells. It is self-dual: D(24-cell) ≅ 24-cell. Its governance margin is zero (Theorem 3.2)."
        },
        {
          "p": 81,
          "text": "Its cells are octahedra. The octahedron is non-self-dual (its dual is the cube), so each cell has μ > 0. The 24-cell is therefore a zero-margin whole composed of positive-margin parts (Theorem 4.1, case iii)."
        },
        {
          "p": 82,
          "text": "The 24-cell has no 3D analog. The Platonic solids contain exactly one self-dual member (the tetrahedron), and the tetrahedron is a simplex. The 24-cell is the unique self-dual non-simplex regular convex polytope in any dimension: in 3D, the only self-dual Platonic solid is the tetrahedron (a simplex); in 5D and above, the only self-dual regular convex polytope is the n-simplex. The 24-cell exists only in 4D."
        },
        {
          "p": 83,
          "text": "This means the 24-cell exists only at the terminal depth of the pentagonal carrier recursion. It is a depth-3 phenomenon with no depth-2 precursor and no depth-4 continuation. The collapse of governance margin (positive-margin parts composing into zero-margin whole) occurs exclusively at the terminal recursive depth and exclusively through a non-simplex self-dual polytope that appears nowhere else in the classification."
        },
        {
          "p": 84,
          "text": "8. The TRIAD-Recursion Correspondence"
        },
        {
          "p": 85,
          "text": "The pentagonal carrier recursion has depth 3 (Theorem 1.1). The TRIAD has three irreducible modalities: □G (genealogical), □S (structural), □F (functional). These cardinalities are identical."
        },
        {
          "p": 86,
          "text": "The two derivations are independent. The recursion depth is a consequence of the Coxeter classification of regular convex polytopes, which is a theorem of classical geometry depending on no axiom of the MaL framework. The TRIAD cardinality is a consequence of Axiom 1, which asserts irreducibility of three modalities of projection and depends on no fact about polytope classification."
        },
        {
          "p": 87,
          "text": "The correspondence admits a structural reading. CARRIER(1) is the pentagon, the semantic domain: the unit of what-is-structured (□S). CARRIER(2) is the dodecahedron, the carrier of Π: the record of where-structure-came-from (□G). CARRIER(3) is the 120-cell, the distributed-projection topology: the consequence of what-structure-does-when-iterated (□F). The three levels of the recursion correspond to the three modalities of the TRIAD."
        },
        {
          "p": 88,
          "text": "The depth-1 anomaly is universal self-duality (every polygon, μ = 0 everywhere). The depth-2 anomaly is the tetrahedron (one self-dual solid among five). The depth-3 anomaly comprises two polytopes (the 5-cell and the 24-cell, of which the 24-cell is the non-simplex anomaly unique to this depth). The anomaly structure grows with depth and produces its most structurally distinctive member at the terminal depth."
        },
        {
          "p": 89,
          "text": "Whether the identity of recursion depth and TRIAD cardinality admits a common-root derivation is an open problem. If the depth-3 termination is derivable from the constitutive conditions of projection (i.e., from the same premises that yield Axiom 1), then Axiom 1 would be a theorem rather than an axiom: the irreducibility of three modalities would follow from the geometry of the carrier recursion. The converse derivation (Axiom 1 implying depth-3 termination) would require a bridge theorem connecting the constitutive conditions of projection to the Coxeter classification. Neither derivation is available. The correspondence is established; the common root remains open."
        },
        {
          "p": 90,
          "text": "9. Consequences"
        },
        {
          "p": 91,
          "text": "C1 (Multi-Agent Bound). The maximum number of governed peers within the pentagonal carrier recursion is 120, coordinated by H₄ symmetry, with trust boundary given by pentagonal face-sharing between adjacent dodecahedral cells. This bound is specific to this recursion and does not exclude coordination geometries outside the regular convex polytope class."
        },
        {
          "p": 92,
          "text": "C2 (Action Composition). The action topology at depth 3 (600-cell) is composed of 600 tetrahedral cells, each with μ = 0. Action at the recursive level is built from constituents that individually cannot separate maintenance from action. Governance resides in the fragility topology (120-cell), not in the action topology (600-cell)."
        },
        {
          "p": 93,
          "text": "C3 (Anomaly Structure). The anomaly count by depth: depth 1 has universal anomaly (every polygon self-dual), depth 2 has one anomaly (tetrahedron), depth 3 has two anomalies (5-cell and 24-cell). The 24-cell, as the unique self-dual non-simplex regular convex polytope in any dimension, is the terminal anomaly of the classification."
        },
        {
          "p": 94,
          "text": "C4 (Termination Equals TRIAD). The pentagonal carrier recursion terminates at depth 3. The TRIAD has 3 irreducible modalities. Independent derivations, identical count. The correspondence is structural, not numerical coincidence: the three levels of the recursion admit a TRIAD reading (□S, □G, □F) that aligns the architectural roles of domain, carrier, and distributed consequence with the three modalities of projection."
        },
        {
          "p": 95,
          "text": "10. Open Problems"
        },
        {
          "p": 96,
          "text": "O1. Explicit computation of the H₄ action on the cell-adjacency graph of the 120-cell (= 1-skeleton of the 600-cell): diameter, expansion constant, chromatic number of the coordination graph."
        },
        {
          "p": 97,
          "text": "O2. Mechanism of μ-collapse in the 24-cell: which operator, in the 15-operator reading, fails under the composition of positive-margin octahedral cells into a zero-margin self-dual whole?"
        },
        {
          "p": 98,
          "text": "O3. The rectified 120-cell as the depth-3 operator manifold, analogous to the icosidodecahedron at depth 2. Does Theorem 12 (Junction Determination) of the Fragility Topology lift to the rectified 120-cell?"
        },
        {
          "p": 99,
          "text": "O4. Common-root theorem: derivation (not observation) connecting the Coxeter classification’s depth-3 termination of the pentagonal family with the TRIAD’s three irreducible modalities. If derivable, Axiom 1 is a theorem."
